.TH S3TEST 1
.SH NAME
s3test \- test the S3 client's decode and network paths
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B s3test
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B S3test
drives
.IR s3 (1)'s
own in-process test seams. SigV4 signing itself is pinned against
published vectors by
.IR awsv4test (1);
this covers the client around it.
.TP
.B listparsetest
(offline) the ListObjectsV2 key extraction and the Error-document
Code/Message pull, over hand-built bodies in S3's real shape.
.TP
.B badauthtest
(live) a real SigV4-signed request with bogus credentials to real
S3, which must come back as AWS's own signed error
.RB ( InvalidAccessKeyId
or
.BR SignatureDoesNotMatch ).
That single exchange proves the whole path end to end \(em the
request was signed, reached S3 over this tree's own TLS, was parsed
by AWS, and its credential checked \(em without any real account, the
same way
.IR bsky (1)/\c
.IR masto (1)'s
bogus-auth seams do.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/s3test.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR s3 (1),
.IR awsv4test (1),
.IR awsv4 (2),
.IR https (2)
.SH BUGS
The live leg needs the real network and reaches
.BR s3. us-east-1\c
.BR .amazonaws.com ;
a failure can mean the world changed rather than the code. Nothing
here writes or reads a real object (that would need real
credentials), so the success path of
.B get\c
/\c
.B put
past authentication is not exercised \(em see
.IR s3 (1)'s
own BUGS.
